ions behind a clean, asynchronous provider protocol (`agent/providers/base.py`):\n\n```python\nclass LLMProvider(ABC):\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def complete(self, prompt: str, system: str | None = None, **kwargs) -> CompletionResult:\n        \"\"\"Execute a text completion or FIM generation.\"\"\"\n\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def chat(self, messages: list[ChatMessage], **kwargs) -> ChatResult:\n        \"\"\"Execute a multi-turn conversation with tool calling.\"\"\"\n\n    @abstractmethod\n    async def embed(self, texts: list[str]) -> list[list[float]]:\n        \"\"\"Generate vector embeddings for semantic search.\"\"\"\n```\n\n<a id=\"supported-runtimes\"></a>\n### Supported Providers Matrix\n\n| Provider Key | Primary Use Cases | Streaming Support | Function / Tool Calling | Network Boundary |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| **`openai`** | Complex planning, synthesis | Yes | Yes (Native JSON Schema) | `egress_llm_net` |\n| **`gemini`** | Large context specs, vision | Yes | Yes (Native Function Calling) | `egress_llm_net` |\n| **`ollama`** | Air-gapped workstations, privacy | Yes | Yes (Model dependent) | `data_net` / local |\n| **`cli_pipe`** | Deterministic CI agents, bash-less | No (Batch) | Pipe JSON protocol | `sandbox_net` (nsjail) |\n| **`remote_runner`** | Forensic code audits, test gates | Yes (WSS) | Streamable RPC | Dedicated mTLS |\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"execution-harnesses\"></a>\n## Zero-Shell CLI Pipe Harnesses (`agy -p` / `codex -p`)\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Engine as AI Orchestrator\n    participant Sandbox as nsjail Sandbox Runner\n    participant Model as CLI Pipe (agy -p / codex -p)\n\n    Engine->>Sandbox: Spawn isolated container (CLONE_NEWNET, read-only FS, 512MB RAM)\n    Engine->>Sandbox: Pipe prompt.json over STDIN\n    Sandbox->>Model: Execute deterministic harness without shell/bash\n    Model->>Sandbox: Generate tool call / response JSON over STDOUT\n    Sandbox-->>Engine: Structured JSON stream (Zero bash exposure)\n    Engine->>Engine: Validate response against Pydantic schema\n```\n\nAutonomous agents frequently need to inspect code, run validators, or check commit history. However, granting an LLM arbitrary bash or shell access introduces severe remote code execution (RCE) hazards.\n\n\ud83d\udc4dkanban resolves this with **Zero-Shell CLI Pipes**:\n- Agents do not receive an interactive shell or bash process.\n- Execution communicates strictly over standard input (`stdin`) and standard output (`stdout`) using structured JSON pipes.\n- Commands run inside a sandboxed Linux jail (`nsjail` / `setpriv`):\n  - Read-only root filesystem.\n  - Network namespace disabled (`CLONE_NEWNET`).\n  - Memory limit capped at 512 MB.\n  - Execution time limited to 15 seconds.\n\n```bash\n# Example invocation of pipe harness:\ncat prompt.json | agy -p --sandbox-jail > response.json\n```\n\n---\n\n## AI Providers and Token Caps Studio (`/admin/ai`)\n\nWorkspace and server administrators manage LLM provider registrations, write-only API key custody, monthly token budgets, and zero-egress policies in the visual studio at `/admin/ai`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    subgraph AIStudio[\"AI Providers & Token Caps Architecture\"]\n        AdminUser[\"Administrator (AAL2)\"] --> Reg[\"Register / Edit Provider\"]\n        Reg --> ProvidersList[\"Configured Providers<br/>- OpenAI (Cloud, priority 10)<br/>- Gemini (Cloud, priority 20)<br/>- Ollama (Local Air-Gap, priority 5)\"]\n        \n        ProvidersList --> AirGapCheck{\"Air-Gap Policy Active?\"}\n        AirGapCheck -->|Yes| BlockCloud[\"Cloud Providers Blocked<br/>Only Local Zero-Egress Ollama Allowed\"]\n        AirGapCheck -->|No| AllowCloud[\"Dispatched by Priority Tier\"]\n        \n        AllowCloud --> BudgetGate{\"Monthly Token Budget Gate\"}\n        BlockCloud --> BudgetGate\n        \n        BudgetGate -->|Within Cap| Exec[\"Execute Completion / Tool Call\"]\n        BudgetGate -->|Cap Exceeded| Reject[\"429 Budget Exceeded<br/>Alert Admin to Reset or Expand Cap\"]\n    end\n```\n\n![AI Providers and Token Caps Studio](images/43-admin-ai-studio-providers.png)\n\n### Key Provider Governance Features:\n1. **Write-Only API Key Custody:** API keys are submitted over encrypted TLS, stored encrypted in the database, and never echoed back to the browser.\n2. **Enterprise Air-Gap Mode:** When activated, all cloud egress to external AI services is blocked at the network layer. Only local, on-premise runtimes (such as Ollama) are authorized for completions.\n3. **Monthly Token Budgets & Ledgers:** Each provider configuration enforces monthly input/output token caps with automated rollover and audit ledger accounting.\n\n---\n\n## Agent Definitions Studio (`/admin/agents`)\n\nAdministrators define and customize autonomous agent personas, tool permissions, and system prompt templates at `/admin/agents`.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph AgentDefFlow[\"Agent Definition & Validation Lifecycle\"]\n        Def[\"Agent Definition<br/>Name, Slug, Model, Prompt Template\"] --> Scope[\"Tool Allowlist Scoping<br/>Permitted MCP Tools (read_ticket, add_comment, etc.)\"]\n        Scope --> Modal[\"Validation Modal (/admin/agents)<br/>Simulate test prompt against agent schema\"]\n        Modal --> Verdict{\"Validation Passes?\"}\n        Verdict -->|Yes| Active[\"Registered as Active Agent Principal\"]\n        Verdict -->|No| Fix[\"Report Schema / Prompt Errors for Correction\"]\n    end\n```\n\n![Agent Definitions Studio Overview](images/44-admin-agent-definitions.png)\n\n![Agent Validation Modal](images/60-agent-validation-modal.png)\n\n### Definition Capabilities:\n- **Role and Model Mapping:** Bind specific personas (e.g. `reality-checker`, `security-auditor`, `code-reviewer`) to optimal models (Gemini 2.5 Flash, GPT-4o, DeepSeek-R1).\n- **Tool Allowlist Scoping:** Strictly restrict which MCP tools each agent principal can invoke, enforcing least privilege.\n- **Interactive Validation Modal:** Test and validate agent definitions before deploying them to live project workflows.\n\n---\n\n## Agent Runners Studio and Distributed mTLS Daemon (`/admin/agent-runners`)\n\nFor heavy build verification, sandbox test execution, and isolated code review, \ud83d\udc4dkanban deploys distributed **Agent Runners** managed through `/admin/agent-runners`.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    actor Admin as Server Administrator\n    participant Studio as Runner Studio (/admin/agent-runners)\n    participant Server as \ud83d\udc4dkanban Orchestrator\n    participant Daemon as Remote Runner Daemon\n\n    Admin->>Studio: Click \"+ Mint Runner Token\"\n    Studio->>Server: Generate ephemeral enrollment token & mTLS cert\n    Server-->>Studio: Return copyable docker/cli run snippet\n    Admin->>Daemon: Launch daemon with enrollment token\n    Daemon->>Server: Establish mTLS WebSocket handshake\n    Server->>Server: Verify cryptographic certificate fingerprint\n    Server-->>Daemon: Channel verified: Runner enters \"ONLINE\"\n    Daemon->>Server: Heartbeat ping every 15s (CPU, RAM, active jobs)\n```\n\n![Agent Runner Studio Overview](images/45-admin-runner-studio.png)\n\n![Runner Mint Token Modal](images/61-runner-mint-token-modal.png)\n\n### Runner Infrastructure:\n- **mTLS Mutual Authentication:** Cryptographic X.509 mutual TLS guarantees that only authorized worker nodes connect to the orchestrator.\n- **Copyable Enrollment Snippets:** One-click copyable `docker run` and CLI commands for instant daemon deployment on build hosts.\n- **Heartbeat Monitoring:** Real-time visibility into runner health, active execution count, CPU load, and container status.\n\n---\n\n## Agent Revocation and Security Controls\n\nWhen an agent session exhibits anomalous activity or a credential leak is detected, administrators can invoke an instant Kill Switch across all surfaces:\n\n![Agent Kill Switch and Immediate Session Revocation](images/18-agent-kill-switch.png)\n\n---\n\n## Remote mTLS Repository Runners\n\nFor long-running verification tasks (e.g. compiling large C++ or Rust binaries, running full end-to-end Playwright suites), tasks are dispatched to external **mTLS Runners**:\n- **Mutual TLS Authentication (mTLS):** Both the \ud83d\udc4dkanban server and the runner verify cryptographic X.509 certificates.\n- **WebSocket Streaming:** Real-time log output and build artifacts stream back over encrypted WebSocket channels.\n- **Read-Only Volume Mounting:** The repository is mounted read-only to prevent unauthorized code mutations on the build host.\n\n---\n\n## Testable AI: Pluggable Fixtures and CLI Validation\n\nAI agent workflows can be notoriously difficult to test deterministically in CI pipelines due to model drift, token costs, and rate limits. \ud83d\udc4dkanban enforces **100% Deterministic AI Testing**:\n\n### Pluggable AI Response Fixtures\nIn test suites, providers are swapped with `FixtureLLMProvider` and `FixtureCLIRunner`:\n- Replays recorded, deterministic JSON responses without making network calls.\n- Simulates network timeouts, rate limit errors (`429`), and malformed responses to verify system resilience.\n\n### Local Agent CLI Diagnostics\nEngineers can validate transition helpers and AI workflows locally via the command line:\n\n```bash\n# Validate a transition helper against a ticket fixture\nkanban-agent validate-transition \\\n  --ticket-file test_ticket.json \\\n  --from-state in_progress \\\n  --to-state done \\\n  --dry-run\n```\n\n---\n\n## Multi-Provider Benchmark & Cutoff Timeout Guide\n\nEvery AI provider configuration and agent definition exposes a configurable hard cutoff timeout (`timeout_ms`, `1000` to `300000` ms). At runtime, `GateWorkerRunner` enforces `cutoff_ms = min(provider.timeout_ms, agent.timeout_ms)`. When an upstream model exceeds `cutoff_ms`, the worker raises `GATE_TIMEOUT` and **fails closed**, leaving the ticket in its source state with zero hung workers.\n\n### Live Full-Stack In-Transition Benchmark Matrix (`In Progress -> Done`)\n\nMeasured end-to-end against `kanban-liveqa-2` (embedded PostgreSQL 16 + `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`) using `StrictVerdictSchema` (`kb-transition-verdict/v1`) on a Positive software ticket (`KAN-1`) and a Negative impossible/unverified ticket (`KAN-2: \"Build a rocket ship and serve ice cream on Saturn\"`):\n\n| Runtime & Model | Cutoff (`timeout_ms`) | Positive (`KAN-1`) | Positive Latency | Negative (`KAN-2`) | Negative Latency | Operational Guidance |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :---: | :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| **Google Gemini** (`gemini-2.5-flash-lite`) | `20000` | `approve` (`Done`) | `3,215 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `1,594 ms` | Fastest overall cloud model with strict schema compliance and `v1beta` AI Studio routing. |\n| **OpenAI Cloud** (`gpt-4o-mini`) | `20000` | `approve` (`Done`) | `3,846 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `2,224 ms` | Fast cloud fallback with strict JSON schema adherence. |\n| **Local Ollama Supercomputer** (`gemma4:e2b`) | `25000` | `approve` (`Done`) | `19,892 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `5,213 ms` | Strong air-gapped 2B edge choice; `20000`-`25000 ms` cutoff recommended. |\n| **Local Ollama GPU/CPU** (`gemma3:4b`) | `20000` / `35000` | `timed_out` (20s) / `approve` (35s) | `20,929 ms` / `31,431 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `16,630 ms` | If VRAM is shared with another pinned model (CPU fallback), set `timeout_ms >= 35000`. |\n| **Local Ollama GPU/CPU** (`granite3.3:2b`) | `20000` | `timed_out` (fail-closed) | `20,321 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `12,691 ms` | Warm-keep or `25000 ms` cutoff avoids cold-load timeout on long prompts. |\n| **Local Ollama Reasoning** (`nemotron-3-super:latest`) | `5000` (Strict) | N/A | N/A | `GATE_TIMEOUT` (fail-closed) | `6,023 ms` | Validates hard 5s cutoff protection with clean fail-closed veto. |\n| **Local Ollama Reasoning** (`nemotron-3-super:latest`) | `45000` (Extended) | `approve` (`Done`) | `31,940 ms` | `reject` (`In Progress`) | `26,502 ms` | Heavy reasoning models (`<think>` tags stripped) should use `45000`-`60000 ms`. |\n\n### Choosing a Cutoff Timeout\n- **Fast Cloud Models (`gpt-4o-mini`, `gemini-2.5-flash-lite`):** `15000` to `20000` ms.\n- **Local 2B-4B Edge Models (`gemma4:e2b`, `gemma3:4b`, `granite3.3:2b`):** `25000` to `35000` ms (covers occasional model reload or shared VRAM).\n- **Heavy Reasoning Models (`nemotron-3-super:latest`, `deepseek-r1`):** `45000` to `60000` ms, or pair with a strict `5000`-`10000` ms cutoff on latency-sensitive columns so stuck calls fail closed immediately.\n\n<a id=\"remote-runners\"></a>\n<a id=\"repository-access\"></a>\n"
